  "generated_text": "To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\n\\]\n\nFirst, we eliminate the fractions by finding a common denominator. The common denominator for \\(x\\) and \\(3\\) is \\(3x\\). We rewrite the equation with a common denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 3 + x \\cdot x}{3x} = b\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{9 + x^2}{3x} = b\n\\]\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by \\(3x\\) to clear the denominator:\n\n\\[\n9 + x^2 = 3bx\n\\]\n\nRearranging the equation to form a standard quadratic equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0\\) to have exactly one solution, its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our equation \\(x^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0\\), we have \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -3b\\), and \\(c = 9\\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = (-3b)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the discriminant:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9b^2 - 36\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have exactly one solution, the discriminant must be zero:\n\n\\[\n9b^2 - 36 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n9b^2 = 36\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nTaking the positive square root (since we need the positive value of \\(b\\)):\n\n\\[\nb = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\(b\\) such that the equation \\(\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\\) will have exactly one solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
